{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793471968,"nonce":"zZyV15CLQdRVW0Bg4sMt1pAaBFU=","message":"Committed in cb96871 and b5652c6. Resize: RESIZE (viewer to app) asks for a picture size and the app answers with a new HELLO; pictures carry their own size so a resize cannot diff against the old one. The tab measures its box times the display scale and asks once the size has held for three looks; a screen size dropdown (aspect-ratio icon and the current ratio as the trigger) offers 16:9 (default), 4:3, 1:1, 9:16 and Fit. The Heaps scene keeps the stage size the app laid itself out for and is scaled and centred in the picture; Scene.drawTo ignores the scene's scale mode, so the shim applies the scale and offset itself and converts pointer positions back. The engine is resized synchronously along with the window. The viewer's tick is resilient (an error shows in the header) and the heaps-surface.sh build is replaced atomically. Verified: the full check suite passes; the real viewer offscreen with the real app (resize to 946x532, 59 frames/s, input applied); a fake viewer with resizes at 0, 0.3, 0.6 s and later plus pointer input. Found along the way: with ashui frame latency 2 (a private copy of the pinned ashui with an env override, not committed anywhere) the viewer drew 58 to 61 frames/s with a 9 ms tick and felt smoother, but still showed 25 to 70 ms hiccups that look like GC pauses; waiting on ashui 6e22681 and a pin bump. First blank view was not reproduced; likely my own test runs rewriting the .hl in place under a running app, now prevented. Not done: a reflow-at-actual-size mode for responsive apps, OpenFL, Flixel, Lime and Ceramic producers, hosted previews through the session, GC pause measurement (ASH_GC_STATS), Linux and Windows hdlls.","files":null}]}